    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nTo evaluate a large exponent, we first write out the power using multiplication signs as usual. Then, we calculate the product by splitting it into groups of two-digit multiplications, before calculating the resulting product.\n\nContent:\nFor example, let's evaluate [MATH: 2^4.]\n\nContent:\nFirst, we write\n[MATH: 4]\n\nContent:\nSince [MATH: 2 \\times 2 = 4,] we have\n[MATH: \\underbrace{2 \\times 2}_{\\large \\color{blue}4} \\times \\underbrace{2 \\times 2}_{\\large \\color{blue}4} = {\\color{blue}4} \\times {\\color{blue}4}.]\n\nContent:\nFinally, we work out [MATH: 4\\times 4.]\nSince [MATH: 4\\times 4=16,] we conclude that [MATH: 2^4=16.]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Squaring Two-Digit Numbers\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: 31^2.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo evaluate [MATH: 31^2,] we start by writing it out with a multiplication sign:\n[MATH: 2]\n\nThen, we work out [MATH: 31 \\times 31.]\n\n[MATH: 3|1; \u00d7|0|0|3|1; 3|1; +|9|3|0; 9|6|1]\n\nTherefore, [MATH: 31 \\times 31 = 961.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 15^{2} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 30]\nb. [MATH: 355]\nc. [MATH: 200]\nd. [MATH: 225]\ne. [MATH: 125]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 26^{2} =][FREE_ENTRY 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Squaring Numbers Ending in Zeros\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: 220^2.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo evaluate [MATH: 220^2,] we start by writing it out with a multiplication sign:\n[MATH: 2]\n\nThen, we work out [MATH: 220 \\times 220.]\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero part and the block of zeros for both numbers:\n[MATH: 220|\u27f6220; 220|\u27f6220]\n\nWe can see that there are [MATH: 2] zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n[MATH: 2|2; \u00d7|0|0|2|2; 0|4|4; +|4|4|0; 4|8|4]\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n[MATH: {\\color{red}484}{\\color{blue}00}= 48,400]\n\nTherefore, [MATH: 220^2 = 48,400.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 60^{2} =][FREE_ENTRY 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 200^{2} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 4, 000]\nb. [MATH: 400]\nc. [MATH: 40, 000]\nd. [MATH: 20, 000]\ne. [MATH: 8, 000]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Raising Numbers to Larger Exponents\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: 7^4.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nAccording to the definition,\n[MATH: 4]\n\nSince [MATH: 7 \\times 7 = 49,] we have\n[MATH: \\underbrace{7 \\times 7}_{\\color{blue}49} \\times \\underbrace{7 \\times 7}_{\\color{blue}49} = {\\color{blue}49} \\times {\\color{blue}49}.]\n\nFinally, we work out [MATH: 49 \\times 49.]\n\n[MATH: [math]\\color{blue}3[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}8[/math]; 4|9; \u00d7|0|0|4|9; 4|4|1; +|1|9|6|0; 2|4|0|1]\n\nTherefore, [MATH: 7^4=2,401.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 3^{4} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 3, 333]\nb. [MATH: 12]\nc. [MATH: 81]\nd. [MATH: 7]\ne. [MATH: 333]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 4^{4} =][FREE_ENTRY 1]"
